# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
#
# TSan suppressions for the nightly `tsan` lane (issue #702, docs/specs/memory-model-v1.md
# section 10).
#
# Format: one entry per line, `interceptor:pattern` (or `race:pattern` for a reported race),
# preceded by a `#` comment line that gives the reason. The sanitizer runtime ignores blank lines and
# `#` comments.
#
# POLICY - read before adding a line:
#   * Tessera's own code is NEVER suppressed here. A race in our code is a bug to fix; the lane
#     exists to surface it.
#   * Only a third-party library may be listed, and only with a reason that names the library, the
#     report it emits and why the report is not ours to fix.
#
# CURRENT STATE: two entries, both for Mesa's lavapipe software Vulkan driver and the LLVM it JITs
# through. They are the only third-party reports the full suite produces; everything else the lane
# found was Tessera's own code and was fixed rather than suppressed.

# Mesa lavapipe (libvulkan_lvp.so), the software Vulkan ICD the Linux lane renders with. Its
# `llvmpipe` worker threads tear down a mutex while another worker still locks it, which TSan
# reports as a data race between `pthread_mutex_destroy` and `pthread_mutex_lock`. Both racing
# accesses are inside `libvulkan_lvp.so`; Tessera appears in the report only as the caller that
# created the device and the threads. It is not ours to fix, and it is not a Tessera defect: the
# same driver is used by every GPU test on this lane, so without the entry 62 of 724 tests fail on
# driver-internal noise and the lane cannot report a real finding. Matched on the module name so a
# race whose *Tessera* frames are involved is still reported.
race:libvulkan_lvp.so

# LLVM's APFloat, reached through llvmpipe's JIT (libLLVM.so.21.1). Two `llvmpipe` workers race on
# an `IEEEFloat` buffer: one reads it through `bitwiseIsEqual` while another is still writing the
# block `llvm::allocate_buffer` handed out. Both racing accesses are inside `libLLVM.so.21.1`; the
# only Tessera frame in the report is the device construction that started the workers. Same
# reasoning as the entry above: third-party, not ours to fix, and it only becomes visible once the
# lavapipe entry stops `halt_on_error=1` at the first report.
race:libLLVM.so
